        answer:
          "Objection: Sextus says that when premisses yield a conclusion, the conclusion may be treated as present among the premisses though unstated. That licenses using a derived assertible as if it were a premiss; it says nothing about stating shared premisses once, and 'implicitly present' is about the conclusion, not about duplicates. Reading it as cut[any,any,merge] builds contraction into a rule whose text does not mention repetition, and then reporting that 'every fit needs contraction' is partly circular: one of the fitting candidates has contraction by construction. Mates conjectured only that the theorem might be the second thema, and conceded it might restate the third. Reply: (1) NOTES.md Phase 3 labels this 'the most interpretive choice in Phase 3' and says the reading is the project's own; it is not presented as Mates' or Sextus'. (2) The finding does not rest on that candidate: Mates without the merging cut also fits, under the set view, and the set view is a parameter, not a rule; contraction is forced by S011 and S012 whichever mechanism supplies it, since under list and multiset with a non-merging cut both are proven undergenerated (lone-atom invariant for S011). (3) If a conclusion may be treated as among the premisses without being stated, then premisses it was derived from need not be restated either, which is what 'merge' encodes; the reading is a construal of 'unstated', not an addition. What would settle it: a Bobzien candidate with her T2 (implicit reuse of a premiss) and T1+, to see whether local contraction suffices for S011 and S012 without the set view; and the Greek of M 8.231 construed for whether 'present among the premisses' is about the conclusion only.",
