    title: "Beyond the syllogistic",
    briefing:
      "Diogenes Laertius 7.78 divides conclusive arguments into the syllogistic and those 'conclusive in the specific sense', which draw their conclusion but not by syllogism. The second class is where the limits of Chrysippus' system show. It holds the subsyllogistic arguments, which differ from syllogisms only in wording; the unmethodically concluding arguments, which are valid but owe their validity to the matter of their terms rather than to a form the themata recognise; and, on the far side of the line, Aristotle's categorical syllogisms, which the Stoics could regiment but not reproduce. The regimentation uses the indefinite conditional, 'if something is a man, that one is an animal', which Sextus M 11.8 to 11 says is how the Stoics wrote a universal. That device raises the question of how much generality the system could express: Bobzien and Shogry argue, in a paper this course holds only in summary, that Stoic logic handled multiple generality through scope conventions on indefinite assertibles, which would put it far ahead of where the standard histories place it. The Nobody paradox (DL 7.82, 7.187), on which Chrysippus wrote eight books, is the test case, since its fallacy lives in exactly the construction the generality claim rests on. The session is hard because the texts are Peripatetic (Alexander), medical (Galen) or doxographical, Galen is absent from the corpus, and the strongest modern claim is one the student cannot yet read.",
